# @PORT_TITLE@

A port of @PORT_TITLE@ on XenoForge's SDK: the game's code recompiled ahead of
time from your own disc and linked with XenoForge's runtime (kernel, GPU on
Vulkan, sound, input), with this repository's own code on top. No part of the
game is in the repository: the build reads your disc image.

## Platforms

| preset | host | builds on |
|---|---|---|
| `windows` | Windows x86-64 | Windows |
| `linux` | Linux x86-64 (or ARM64, built natively there) | Linux |
| `linux-arm64` | Linux ARM64, cross-compiled | Linux x86-64 |
| `android-arm64` | Android arm64-v8a (`libplayer_@PORT_NAME@.so`, SDL's activity starts it) | any, with the NDK |

Each takes an SDK built for its host (`XENOFORGE_SDK`). The cross presets
also take the build machine's `xfrecomp` (`XENOFORGE_HOST_XFRECOMP`: the
recompiler runs during the build), and Android takes the NDK
(`ANDROID_NDK_HOME`).

## Building

You need XenoForge's SDK installed (`cmake --install <XenoForge's build>
--component sdk --prefix <dir>`), clang, CMake 3.25+ and Ninja.

```
cmake --preset windows -DXENOFORGE_SDK=<the SDK's prefix> -DGAME_ISO=<your disc image>
cmake --build --preset windows
```

(`linux` on Linux; or `cmake --preset local` and `cmake --build --preset
local`, with this machine's paths in `CMakeUserPresets.json`, which is not
versioned). The first build recompiles the disc: several minutes. Each
preset builds in `build/<preset>/`.

## Running

```
build/<preset>/player_@PORT_NAME@ <disc image>                 # the game in a window
build/<preset>/boot_@PORT_NAME@ <disc image> --seconds 20      # headless, with a report
```

Both take XenoForge's usual command line (its `docs/runtime.md`) and
`--option <key>=<value>` for this port's options.

## Layout

| path | what |
|---|---|
| `src/main.cpp` | the port's main: its name, options, `on_init` |
| `src/hooks.cpp` | hooks on the game's functions, reloaded while it runs (linked in on Android) |
| `project.toml` | the recompiler's project file: names, hooked functions, fixes to discovery |
